{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["The sense of <b>remain a widow in your father's house</b> is that she should conduct her widowhood there. [The phrase] \"until Shelah grows up\" hinted to her: mourn and don garments of mourning and do not anoint yourself with oil, like a woman girded with sackcloth for the husband of her youth, until Shelah grows up and performs yibum with you — for such is the practice of a woman awaiting yibum. For a woman who goes out with the intent to marry a strange man dons garments of mourning for a few days according to custom, then takes comfort, puts on ordinary garments, covers herself with the head-veil, and beautifies herself until she is married to the man." "<b>For he said</b> — that is to say, he was putting her off with a pretext; he had no intention of giving her to him [Shelah], \"for he said, lest he too die like his brothers,\" for she is confirmed as one whose husbands die — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 38:11). But I do not know why Judah, who was a ruler in his generation, would be ashamed of this woman and not say to her, \"Go in peace from my house,\" and why he would deceive her when she is forbidden to Shelah, as they said (Yevamot 64a) regarding marriage: after two occasions it becomes an established presumption. Moreover, since he was insistent about her harlotry to the point of holding her liable to burning, it appears that he wanted her to remain in his house. And it is far-fetched that Judah did not know that his sons had sinned and that he sent them by the hand of their transgression while Tamar bore no sin in them. And the correct view in my eyes is that Shelah was fit to perform yibum, but his father did not want him to perform yibum with her while he was still a youth, lest he sin with her like his brothers who died in their youth — for they were youths, none of them being even twelve years old; and when he would grow up and heed his father's discipline, then he would give her to him as a wife. And when she waited many days and saw that Shelah had grown up in her eyes — yet he was still a youth to his father, being not even ten years old, and his father was still waiting further — then Tamar hastened, out of the intensity of her desire to bear offspring from the holy seed, and did this deed."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:43.227952428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}